<BlogPost slug="gas-station-heroin-what-is-it-and-is-it-dangerous" toc={toc}>
  <p>Despite its name, gas station heroin is not heroin at all&mdash;it's tianeptine, a synthetic drug with <a href="/what-we-treat/opioids/">opioid-like effects</a>. Its street name comes from being widely available at gas stations, where people go to buy illicit substances. It can also be purchased online and in convenience stores.</p>
  <p>Gas station heroin is highly addictive and can quickly lead to physical dependence, even among people who haven't used substances before. It can also be life-threatening. Tianeptine is not FDA-approved for any use in the U.S., and a growing number of states have banned or restricted it. Poison control center cases involving tianeptine increased from 11 total between 2000 and 2013 to 151 in 2020 alone. By 2023, that number had climbed to 391 cases nationwide.</p>

  <figure class="bp-infographic">
    <img src="/images/blog/infographics/tianeptine-case-growth.svg" alt="Bar chart showing U.S. poison control center cases involving tianeptine rising from 11 total between 2000 and 2013, to 151 in 2020, to 391 in 2023" loading="lazy" width="760" height="300" />
    <figcaption>Poison control cases involving tianeptine have climbed sharply since 2020.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>Below, I explain what gas station heroin is, what effects it has, and what treatment is available for it.</p>

  <h2 id="what-is">What Is Gas Station Heroin?</h2>
  <p>Gas station heroin is a colloquial name for tianeptine. In the U.S. it is not FDA-approved and has no accepted medical use; it is unscheduled at the federal level but banned or restricted in several states. In other countries, tianeptine is used as a prescription antidepressant. While it's not FDA-approved for any purpose here, some companies produce products containing it, falsely claiming it can treat anxiety and depression and provide pain relief.</p>
  <p>Gas station heroin is often sold as part of dietary supplements and mood enhancers. Other names for gas station heroin include:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Tia</li>
    <li>ZaZa</li>
    <li>Tianaa</li>
    <li>Pegasus</li>
    <li>Neptune's Fix</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="effects">What Are the Effects of Gas Station Heroin?</h2>
  <p>At the low doses used for prescription therapy abroad (about 25&ndash;50 mg/day), tianeptine acts mainly on glutamate and serotonin signaling. However, at the much higher doses seen in misuse, it acts as a mu-opioid receptor agonist in the brain, causing effects similar to other opioids such as heroin, oxycodone, or morphine. Like those drugs, gas station heroin creates a euphoric "high" and sedation, and can produce the same physical and behavioral <a href="/blog/signs-of-pain-medication-addiction-what-to-know/">warning signs seen in other opioid addictions</a>.</p>
  <p>In overdose, gas station heroin produces an opioid toxidrome and can be life-threatening, especially at high doses. Withdrawal produces a different, but also opioid-like, picture. Tianeptine is highly addictive and can result in a substance use disorder (SUD). In one case, a person who had been using tianeptine for two weeks, taking 10&ndash;15 pills every day, had to take the drug every four to six hours to prevent withdrawal symptoms.</p>

  <figure class="bp-infographic">
