  <h2 id="dangerous">Is It Dangerous?</h2>
  <p>Yes, gas station heroin is dangerous. This year, someone at a gas station died from an overdose in Fayette County, Pennsylvania, while a woman elsewhere in the state died after accidentally overdosing on tianeptine. Years earlier, in 2018, two men in Texas died in the first known tianeptine fatalities in the United States, after obtaining tianeptine powder online.</p>
  <p>Because gas station heroin is unregulated, it's impossible to know the exact number of fatalities and injuries it has caused. What is known is that it can worsen existing mental health disorders&mdash;one case report described a young man with major depressive disorder whose depression and suicidal ideation worsened as his tianeptine use escalated, a risk our <a href="/programs/dual-diagnosis-treatment/">dual diagnosis program</a> is built to address (see <a href="/blog/what-is-dual-diagnosis/">why treating both conditions together matters</a>).</p>
  <p>Other statistics show 82% of calls about gas station heroin involve men, and nearly 57% involve people aged 21&ndash;40.</p>

  <figure class="bp-infographic">
    <img src="/images/blog/infographics/tianeptine-poison-control-demographics.svg" alt="Two gauge charts showing that 82% of poison control calls about tianeptine involve men, and nearly 57% involve people ages 21 to 40" loading="lazy" width="760" height="260" />
    <figcaption>Most poison control calls about tianeptine involve men in their 20s and 30s.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>On a Reddit forum for people trying to quit tianeptine, previous heavy users of opioids said the drug is more addictive than other substances they've tried. The DEA has found tianeptine powder in bulk, used by drug dealers to make counterfeit hydrocodone and oxycodone pills and to mimic bags of heroin.</p>
  <p>The stories around tianeptine have been enough for the FDA to issue repeat warnings and to ask retailers to stop selling products that contain it.</p>

  <h2 id="treatment">Is There Treatment for Gas Station Heroin Addiction?</h2>
  <p>Yes, treatment is available and effective. <a href="/programs/medical-detox/">Medical detox</a> helps a person safely withdraw from the drug under 24/7 medical supervision, easing symptoms and cravings during the taper. In an acute overdose, naloxone&mdash;a fast-acting opioid antagonist related to <a href="/blog/does-naltrexone-help-with-alcohol-withdrawal/">medications like naltrexone</a>&mdash;can reverse the opioid effects. Afterward, non-narcotic <a href="/programs/medication-assisted-treatment/">Medication-Assisted Treatment</a> such as naltrexone/Vivitrol can help protect against relapse without introducing a new dependence.</p>
  <p>After detox, when a person is physically stable, structured addiction programs including <a href="/programs/residential-treatment/">residential care</a> and outpatient programs continue recovery efforts. These levels of care integrate evidence-based therapies such as Cognitive Behavioral Therapy (CBT), encouraging people to reframe thought patterns that fuel addiction. Activities to promote mindfulness, emotional regulation, and distress tolerance also complement therapies.</p>
  <p>Seeking support for addiction is a display of strength and self-care. Gas station heroin is a drug that can take an immediate hold and have destructive effects. But there is always a route to heal, and this journey can lead to a healthy, fulfilled life.</p>

